    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Is \\(\\log_{2}(10)\\) greater than 3 or less than 3? Is \\(\\log_{10}(2)\\) greater than or less than 1? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How are these two quantities related?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Many relationships that can be expressed with an exponent can also be expressed with a logarithm. Let’s look at this equation: \\(\\displaystyle 2^7 = 128\\) The base is 2 and the exponent is 7, so it can be expressed as a logarithm with base…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "In general, an exponential equation and a logarithmic equation are related as shown here:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Exponents can be negative, so a logarithm can have negative values. For example \\(3^{\\text-4} = \\frac{1}{81}\\), which means that \\(\\log_3 \\frac{1}{81} = \\text-4\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "An exponential equation cannot always be solved by observation. For example, \\(2^x = 19\\) does not have an obvious solution. The logarithm gives us a way to represent the solution to this equation: \\(x=\\log_2 19\\). The expression \\(\\log_2…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U04-L10-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "10.3: Exponential and Logarithmic Forms",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The logarithm to base 10 of a number \\(x\\), written \\(\\log_{10}(x)\\), is the exponent you raise 10 to get \\(x\\), so it is the number \\(y\\) that makes the equation \\(10^y = x\\) true. Logarithms to other bases are defined the same way with…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u04-l10.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
